    `s 32(1) the total of the deductions is within the limit` `the total deducted other than for absence, advances, loans and cooperative societies` `my salary payable for the salary period` NOTHING

-- s 32(1).
@export Under section 32(1), what is the largest total my employer may deduct from my salary in the salary period, in dollars?
GIVEN `my salary payable for the salary period` IS A NUMBER @desc What salary, in dollars, is payable to you for the salary period?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `Under section 32(1), what is the largest total of deductions in the salary period` IS
    `s 32(1) the largest total of deductions in the salary period` `my salary payable for the salary period` NOTHING

@export Under section 32(2), does the limit in section 32(1) apply to a deduction from my last salary?
GIVEN `it is from my last salary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the deduction made from the last salary due to you on the termination or completion of your contract of service?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 32(2), does the limit in section 32(1) apply to a deduction from my last salary` IS
    `s 32(2) subsection (1) applies to the deduction` `it is from my last salary`

-- s 33.  The Minister may prescribe other ceilings; the person cannot state them, so the Act's own are used.
-- Any allowance however described is left out of the salary measured against the ceiling (s 33(1) has the same words as s 35, lines 1657-1670), so allowances are 0 here.
@export Under section 33(1), does the rule that my salary is paid before other debts apply to me?
GIVEN `I am a workman` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a workman (a person doing manual labour, and the other kinds the Act names)?
      `I am employed in a managerial or an executive position` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you employed in a managerial or an executive position?
      `my basic pay for the month` IS A NUMBER @desc What is your basic pay for a month, in dollars?
      `my commissions for the month` IS A NUMBER @desc What commissions do you earn in a month, in dollars?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 33(1), does the rule that my salary is paid before other debts apply to me` IS
    IF `I am a workman`
    THEN `s 33(1)(a) the person is a workman to whom this section applies` `I am a workman` (`s 33(1) the monthly salary measured against the ceiling` (`my monthly pay from my payslip` `my basic pay for the month` `my commissions for the month` 0)) NOTHING
    ELSE `s 33(1)(b) the employee is one to whom this section applies` `I am a workman` `I am employed in a managerial or an executive position` (`s 33(1) the monthly salary measured against the ceiling` (`my monthly pay from my payslip` `my basic pay for the month` `my commissions for the month` 0)) NOTHING

-- s 33(3).  Situation: asked only of a person claiming against the sale of an employer's property or the money owed to the employer.
@export Under section 33(3), does the rule for paying my salary first apply to this property or money?
GIVEN `which property or money` IS A `Which property or money` @desc What is the property or money?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 33(3), does the rule apply to the property or money` IS
    `s 33(3) subsection (2) applies to the property or money` (`my property or money in the section's words` `which property or money`)

@export Under section 33(4), what is the most I can be paid from the sale of my employer's property or the money owed to my employer, in dollars?
GIVEN `the salary owed to me` IS A NUMBER @desc How much salary, in dollars, does your employer owe you?
      `my salary for one month` IS A NUMBER @desc What is your salary for one month, in dollars?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `Under section 33(4), what is the most I can be paid in priority` IS
    `s 33(4) the most payable to the employee under subsection (2)` `the salary owed to me` `my salary for one month`

-- s 33(7).
@export Under section 33(7), am I an employee for the purposes of section 33?
GIVEN `I am an employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you an employee?
      `I am a subcontractor for labour` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a subcontractor for labour?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 33(7), am I an employee for the purposes of section 33` IS
    `s 33(7) the person is an employee for the purposes of this section` `I am an employee` `I am a subcontractor for labour`

-- s 33(7).
@export Under section 33(7), is this sum salary for the purposes of section 33?
GIVEN `the sum is money due to a subcontractor for labour` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the sum money due to a subcontractor for labour?
      `the sum is salary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the sum salary?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 33(7), is the sum salary for the purposes of section 33` IS
    `s 33(7) the sum is salary for the purposes of this section` `the sum is money due to a subcontractor for labour` `the sum is salary`

-- s 34(1).
@export Under section 34(1), is my employer guilty of an offence for failing to pay my salary as Part 3 requires?
GIVEN `my employer fails to pay salary in accordance with Part 3` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does your employer fail to pay your salary in accordance with the provisions of Part 3 of the Act?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 34(1), is my employer guilty of an offence` IS
    `s 34(1) the employer is guilty of an offence` `my employer fails to pay salary in accordance with Part 3`

-- s 34.
@export Under section 34, is my employer guilty of an offence for contravening section 21, 22 or 23?
GIVEN `my employer contravenes section 21, 22 or 23` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does your employer contravene section 21, 22 or 23?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 34, is my employer guilty of an offence under section 21, 22 or 23` IS
    `s 34 the employer is guilty of an offence under s 21, 22 or 23` `my employer contravenes section 21, 22 or 23`

-- s 34(1).  Where the failure is not a contravention of section 21, 22 or 23 the Act points to section 112 for the penalty, and that section is not among the sources, so this question answers NOTHING whatever the facts.
@export Under section 34(1), what is the penalty on conviction where the failure is not a contravention of section 21, 22 or 23?
GIVEN `my employer has been convicted of an offence before` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has your employer been convicted of an offence before?
GIVETH A MAYBE `Penalty on conviction`
DECIDE `Under section 34(1), what is the penalty where the failure is not a contravention of section 21, 22 or 23` IS
    `s 34(1) the penalty on conviction where the failure is not a contravention of section 21, 22 or 23` NOTHING NOTHING `my employer has been convicted of an offence before`

-- s 34.  Situation: asked only of a person whose employer has failed to pay salary as Part 3 requires.
@export Under section 34(2), what is the penalty on my employer, on conviction, for contravening section 21, 22 or 23?
GIVEN `my employer is guilty of an offence under section 34(1) for contravening section 21, 22 or 23` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your employer guilty of an offence under section 34(1) for contravening section 21, 22 or 23?
      `my employer's date of conviction` IS A DATE @desc On what date is your employer convicted or found guilty of this offence?
      `an earlier conviction` IS A DATE @desc On what date was your employer earlier convicted or found guilty of contravening section 21, 22 or 23? (1 January 1900 if never)
GIVETH A MAYBE `Penalty on conviction`
DECIDE `Under section 34(2), what is the penalty on my employer` IS
    `s 34(2) the penalty on conviction` `my employer is guilty of an offence under section 34(1) for contravening section 21, 22 or 23` (`s 34(3) the person is a repeat offender` `my employer's date of conviction` (IF `an earlier conviction` EQUALS Date 1 1 1900 THEN EMPTY ELSE LIST `an earlier conviction`))


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Rest days, hours of work and overtime (Part 4, ss 35 to 41A)`
-- Part 4 applies only to a workman who earns no more than the ceiling, and to another employee, other than
-- one in a managerial or an executive position, who earns no more than the lower ceiling (s 35).
-- Every question below asks the five facts that decide that, and answers NOTHING for a person the Part does not reach.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 35.  The Minister may prescribe other ceilings than $4,500 and $2,600; the person cannot state those, so the Act's own are used.
-- Overtime, bonus, annual wage supplement, productivity incentive payments and any allowance however described are left out of the salary measured against the ceiling, so none of them is asked.
@export Under section 35, does Part 4 of the Act apply to me?
GIVEN `I am a workman` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a workman (a person doing manual labour, and the other kinds the Act names)?
      `I am employed in a managerial or an executive position` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you employed in a managerial or an executive position?
      `my basic pay for a month` IS A NUMBER @desc What is your basic pay for a month, in dollars, before overtime and bonuses?
      `my commissions for a month` IS A NUMBER @desc What commissions do you earn in a month, in dollars?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 35, does Part 4 apply to me` IS
    `Part 4 applies to me from what I say` `I am a workman` `I am employed in a managerial or an executive position` `my basic pay for a month` `my commissions for a month`

-- s 36(1).
@export Under section 36(1), has my employer allowed me the rest day in the week?
GIVEN `I am a workman` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a workman (a person doing manual labour, and the other kinds the Act names)?
      `I am employed in a managerial or an executive position` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you employed in a managerial or an executive position?
      `my basic pay for a month` IS A NUMBER @desc What is your basic pay for a month, in dollars, before overtime and bonuses?
      `my commissions for a month` IS A NUMBER @desc What commissions do you earn in a month, in dollars?
      `I was allowed a rest day of one whole day without pay in the week` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you allowed in the week a rest day of one whole day without pay?
      `the rest day was a Sunday or another day my employer determined` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the rest day a Sunday, or another day your employer determined?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 36(1), has my employer allowed me the rest day` IS
    `if Part 4 applies to me, the yes or no` (`Part 4 applies to me from what I say` `I am a workman` `I am employed in a managerial or an executive position` `my basic pay for a month` `my commissions for a month`) (JUST (`s 36(1) the employer has allowed the employee the rest day in the week` `I was allowed a rest day of one whole day without pay in the week` `the rest day was a Sunday or another day my employer determined`))

-- s 36(2).  Situation: asked only of a person engaged in shift work.
@export Under section 36(2), may my employer substitute a continuous period as my rest day?
GIVEN `I am a workman` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a workman (a person doing manual labour, and the other kinds the Act names)?
      `I am employed in a managerial or an executive position` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you employed in a managerial or an executive position?
      `my basic pay for a month` IS A NUMBER @desc What is your basic pay for a month, in dollars, before overtime and bonuses?
      `my commissions for a month` IS A NUMBER @desc What commissions do you earn in a month, in dollars?
      `I am engaged in shift work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you engaged in shift work?
      `the hours in the continuous period my employer substitutes` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours long is the continuous period that your employer substitutes as your rest day?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 36(2), may my employer substitute a continuous period as my rest day` IS
    `if Part 4 applies to me, the yes or no` (`Part 4 applies to me from what I say` `I am a workman` `I am employed in a managerial or an executive position` `my basic pay for a month` `my commissions for a month`) (`s 36(2) the employer may substitute the continuous period as a rest day` `I am engaged in shift work` `the hours in the continuous period my employer substitutes`)

-- s 36(3).  Situation: asked only of a person engaged in shift work.
@export Under section 36(3), is my rest day deemed to have been granted within the week?
GIVEN `I am a workman` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a workman (a person doing manual labour, and the other kinds the Act names)?
      `I am employed in a managerial or an executive position` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you employed in a managerial or an executive position?
      `my basic pay for a month` IS A NUMBER @desc What is your basic pay for a month, in dollars, before overtime and bonuses?
      `my commissions for a month` IS A NUMBER @desc What commissions do you earn in a month, in dollars?
      `I am engaged in shift work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you engaged in shift work?
      `the continuous period of 30 hours begins on a Sunday` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the continuous period of 30 hours begin on a Sunday?
